WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.700
So the Blackboard component is our equivalent of the behavior tree component for the behavior tree.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.699
The Blackboard component is to the Blackboard.

3
00:00:08.699 --> 00:00:11.699
It's a wrapper around the kind of core object.

4
00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:15.699
So in this case the Blackboard is the thing that the Blackboard component holds inside it

5
00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:18.699
and it gives us a way to interact with that Blackboard from outside it

6
00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:21.699
so we can pass information in, we can query information out of it.

7
00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:25.699
Just as an example, if we were to jump to our project,

8
00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:31.019
if we go into the AI controller, again this is the project you can download with this tutorial.

9
00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:33.520
We don't need to go into this in detail at the moment.

10
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.520
I'll demonstrate this a little bit later on, what this is all actually doing.

11
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.520
But if we look inside our AI controller, when we perceive some actors,

12
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.520
we do a little bit of querying, we check if it's a pawn,

13
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.520
and then this point where we do Blackboard set value as object,

14
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.520
this is reaching into the Blackboard component on the AI controller and setting a value.

15
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:58.520
So this is reaching into our Blackboard, assigning something based on world logic from world data,

16
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.020
and then it is assigned to the Blackboard and then the behavior tree,

17
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.019
which is linked with this Blackboard, can operate with that information,

18
00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:13.019
can behave based on that gameplay change, based on that world data.

19
00:01:13.019 --> 00:01:18.150
So as I mentioned, it's strongly paired with the behavior tree component.

20
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.150
This is really obvious from even just when you go into editing a Blackboard.

21
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:28.469
If I go to find our behavior tree here, and I go to our Blackboard,

22
00:01:28.469 --> 00:01:31.969
okay, from the Blackboard, maybe it's, but if you go to your behavior tree,

23
00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:35.969
this is linked with a Blackboard asset, and you can jump back and forth between the two.

24
00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:40.060
We've got the editor kind of like, you know,

25
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.060
really indicates how strongly these two things are paired together.

26
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:58.700
But even inside the actual code itself, if we were to jump to our Blackboard component,

27
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.700
you will see we've got functions for getting the brain component,

28
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.700
we've got a function for caching the brain component,

29
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.200
so it very much expects this thing to exist.

30
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.199
And even inside our brain component, I think it is,

31
00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:15.199
I don't think it's the behavior tree, it might be the behavior tree, let's see.

32
00:02:15.199 --> 00:02:26.610
behavior-tree-component.cpp, sorry.

33
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:40.780
And I think it's initialize component, cache-behavior.

34
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.780
Maybe it's inside the brain component, one second.

35
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:52.110
brain-component.cpp, yeah, right there.

36
00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.610
So inside our brain component on the initialize component,

37
00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.610
it expects that, you know, whatever owns this component

38
00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:04.110
also has a Blackboard component class, and then it kind of assigns itself

39
00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.110
as the brain for that Blackboard.

40
00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:12.789
So you can see how much these two things expect to kind of work in conjunction with each other.

41
00:03:12.789 --> 00:03:16.289
So yeah, in terms of the actual creation,

42
00:03:16.289 --> 00:03:18.789
it follows a similar pattern to the behavior tree.

43
00:03:18.789 --> 00:03:23.289
So you can already create one on your AI controller,

44
00:03:23.289 --> 00:03:26.789
and then it's kind of already available if you want the AI to work

45
00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:29.289
as soon as it's possessing an object.

46
00:03:29.289 --> 00:03:33.289
And similarly, if you go to run a behavior tree, what that will do is,

47
00:03:33.289 --> 00:03:40.789
as you've seen, you can choose a default Blackboard to use for your behavior tree.

48
00:03:40.789 --> 00:03:43.789
You can just assign the Blackboard this should use.

49
00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:45.789
So if you were to run that behavior tree,

50
00:03:45.789 --> 00:03:49.289
and the AI controller didn't have a behavior tree component already,

51
00:03:49.289 --> 00:03:51.289
it would create the appropriate behavior tree component,

52
00:03:51.289 --> 00:03:54.289
plug in this behavior tree, and then create a Blackboard component

53
00:03:54.289 --> 00:03:58.289
using the default Blackboard of that behavior tree.

54
00:03:58.289 --> 00:04:01.289
So essentially, you can create the Blackboard in two ways.

55
00:04:01.289 --> 00:04:04.289
You can make it ahead of time in editor, or you can rely on that kind of

56
00:04:04.289 --> 00:04:11.229
again creates that runtime by that run behavior tree functionality.

57
00:04:11.229 --> 00:04:14.729
And then, oh, yeah, the last thing to mention is just that

58
00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:18.730
one of the kind of powerful things about Blackboard is the ability for objects

59
00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:21.230
to kind of register themselves as observers.

60
00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:25.730
And what you would essentially do is you Blackboard keys are again,

61
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.230
this kind of variable type.

62
00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:32.230
So it's represent particular data, but it's kind of a struct around that data.

63
00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:35.230
And you register yourself as a listener for a particular Blackboard key

64
00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.730
to be notified when that Blackboard key has changed.

65
00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:42.730
So an example of that is the move to execute task.

66
00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.730
I think I've got that open still.

67
00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.230
Bt task move to.

68
00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:57.430
And if we go to execute task, you'll see here.

69
00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.430
It reaches into the Blackboard component,

70
00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.430
finds, gets the memory, gets the delegate handle,

71
00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:09.519
and basically registers an observer like this.

72
00:05:09.519 --> 00:05:13.519
So essentially, in this case, it's the move to task

73
00:05:13.519 --> 00:05:17.519
might need to be notified if the Blackboard value has changed

74
00:05:17.519 --> 00:05:20.519
either because the thing it's following has changed

75
00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:26.519
or if the actor has moved, we need to be kind of aware of that.

76
00:05:26.519 --> 00:05:29.519
And other examples of that would be,

77
00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:33.519
as we've kind of already mentioned in our behavior tree,

78
00:05:33.519 --> 00:05:38.519
we can have a decorator that's kind of based on a Blackboard condition.

79
00:05:38.519 --> 00:05:41.519
And so therefore, this will need to register itself

80
00:05:41.519 --> 00:05:44.519
as an observer of whatever Blackboard key we choose,

81
00:05:44.519 --> 00:05:46.519
in this case, its target actor,

82
00:05:46.519 --> 00:05:52.519
so that this Blackboard, so that this decorator can essentially be like rerun,

83
00:05:52.519 --> 00:05:55.519
can abort, can do whatever abort behavior it needs to do,

84
00:05:55.519 --> 00:05:59.519
can rerun to check for whether or not the result has changed.

85
00:05:59.519 --> 00:06:04.519
So yeah, that's another place where this logic of registering as an observer

86
00:06:04.519 --> 00:06:06.519
will already be getting utilized.

87
00:06:06.519 --> 00:06:10.259
So we've talked about the kind of behavior logic.

88
00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:13.259
We've talked about the brain, the memory.

89
00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:17.259
But one of the really key components to implementing AI

90
00:06:17.259 --> 00:06:19.259
is getting the AI to move around.

91
00:06:19.259 --> 00:06:27.259
Unreal has its own native implementation of navigation,

92
00:06:27.259 --> 00:06:29.259
which is the navigation system.

93
00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:33.259
So the navigation system is comprised of a mesh

94
00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:36.259
which is generated in the editor.

95
00:06:36.259 --> 00:06:41.259
And essentially this mesh will query things about the environment

96
00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:46.259
and generate a mesh either at runtime or editor time,

97
00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:49.259
typically editor time first,

98
00:06:49.259 --> 00:06:56.259
to basically do a lot of tracing and querying in a geometric sense

99
00:06:56.259 --> 00:07:00.259
to establish what areas are navigable and non-navigable.

100
00:07:00.259 --> 00:07:05.259
It's important to mention that this is particularly for entities

101
00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:06.259
that run along the ground.

102
00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:10.259
So if we wanted something like 3D,

103
00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:15.259
so either for flying around or swimming-based navigation,

104
00:07:15.259 --> 00:07:18.259
it's likely that you'll need to roll your own implementation for that.

105
00:07:18.259 --> 00:07:20.259
It's always worth checking Unreal plugins.

106
00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:22.259
It's always worth looking at FAB

107
00:07:22.259 --> 00:07:25.259
because it's quite likely that something for this already exists.

108
00:07:25.259 --> 00:07:29.259
But certainly the out-of-the-box navigation system, the NavMesh,

109
00:07:29.259 --> 00:07:33.259
is designed for along-the-floor land-based AI movement.

110
00:07:33.259 --> 00:07:36.480
As I said, it already supports static and dynamic,

111
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.480
so we can generate this nav data at editor time,

112
00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.480
or if we need it to be responsive

113
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.480
to things that are changing in the environment,

114
00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.480
we can mark it up as dynamic,

115
00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.480
obviously with a slight performance cost.

116
00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.480
And then we can also use nav modifier volumes,

117
00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.480
which can be used as part of this,

118
00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.480
either statically or dynamically,

119
00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:01.480
and these can incur navigation costs

120
00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.480
if we want areas of high cost,

121
00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.480
and they can also act as blockers

122
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:10.480
to prevent navigation being generated around particular areas.

123
00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.480
Physical objects can also affect this in a similar way.

124
00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.480
Any time you're creating a primitive component,

125
00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:20.480
so typically a collision or a visual mesh,

126
00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.480
you can mark that object

127
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.480
as whether or not it should affect navigation,

128
00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:26.480
and then that would be whether or not

129
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:29.480
essentially the navigation system should pay attention to this mesh

130
00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:32.480
to its bounding volume,

131
00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.480
whether it should pay attention to it

132
00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.480
when it's generating navigable earth space.

133
00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.519
Different navigation can be generated for different agents,

134
00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:45.519
so we can have the idea of large objects and small objects

135
00:08:45.519 --> 00:08:47.519
and them navigating in different ways,

136
00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:49.519
being able to negotiate different spaces.

137
00:08:49.519 --> 00:08:53.519
A motorbike can get through a gap that a tank can't,

138
00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:56.519
and so we can generate different nav meshes

139
00:08:56.519 --> 00:08:58.519
to account for that.

140
00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:02.519
We can also, just whenever we're talking about nav mesh stuff,

141
00:09:02.519 --> 00:09:04.519
it's worth mentioning that we can hit P

142
00:09:04.519 --> 00:09:07.519
whenever we're in editor to visualize this.

143
00:09:07.519 --> 00:09:12.519
This is not when you're in play and editor mode,

144
00:09:12.519 --> 00:09:14.519
or it can be when you're in play and editor mode,

145
00:09:14.519 --> 00:09:15.519
but only when you're ejected,

146
00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:19.519
so not when you're playing as possessing a pawn moving around,

147
00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:22.519
but you can just press P to kind of see the nav mesh

148
00:09:22.519 --> 00:09:23.519
and how it's being generated.

149
00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:25.519
That's either at editor time or at runtime

150
00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:28.029
if you're in eject mode.

151
00:09:28.029 --> 00:09:33.029
Also, we can form links between different islands of nav mesh

152
00:09:33.029 --> 00:09:36.029
to allow AI to traverse spaces.

153
00:09:36.029 --> 00:09:38.029
This can either be from heights,

154
00:09:38.029 --> 00:09:43.029
so we can handle the idea of jumping from a ledge to the ground,

155
00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:49.029
or it can be, as I said, through areas of distinct space

156
00:09:49.029 --> 00:09:51.029
that have been generated.

157
00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:55.799
We can link them together so that AI can traverse between them.

158
00:09:55.799 --> 00:09:58.799
As we've already mentioned, we've covered the bare bones,

159
00:09:58.799 --> 00:10:02.799
but just to go into actually how we would do this,

160
00:10:02.799 --> 00:10:04.799
to actually make a nav mesh in the first place,

161
00:10:04.799 --> 00:10:09.679
if we were just to start a new map.

162
00:10:09.679 --> 00:10:11.679
By default, when you make an Unreal level,

163
00:10:11.679 --> 00:10:13.679
it won't have nav,

164
00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:18.679
but all you have to do to create it is if you go to volumes

165
00:10:18.679 --> 00:10:22.679
and go to nav mesh bounds volume.

166
00:10:22.679 --> 00:10:25.679
As soon as you create that,

167
00:10:25.679 --> 00:10:28.679
you'll see you get this recast nav mesh.

168
00:10:28.679 --> 00:10:34.679
This essentially is the runtime data that represents your navigable space,

169
00:10:34.679 --> 00:10:38.679
whereas it's the volumes that mark out areas that should be navigable.

170
00:10:38.679 --> 00:10:42.679
If I drop that here, you can see the nav mesh is being generated.

171
00:10:42.679 --> 00:10:44.679
You can see the effect of hitting P here.

172
00:10:44.679 --> 00:10:47.679
That's because I was already in show navigation mode.

173
00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:49.679
If you press P, this is what it would be toggling on and off.

174
00:10:49.679 --> 00:10:52.679
We get this kind of data about the nav mesh that has been generated.

175
00:10:52.679 --> 00:10:54.679
We can see that mesh being generated.

176
00:10:54.679 --> 00:11:01.679
You can see if I were to just expand this a little bit or something like that,

177
00:11:01.679 --> 00:11:03.679
that's going to marry up there.

178
00:11:03.679 --> 00:11:06.679
I can also just duplicate this bounds volume,

179
00:11:06.679 --> 00:11:09.679
if I want to say this is navigable space and that.

180
00:11:09.679 --> 00:11:13.679
You'll notice that even though I'm making multiple nav mesh bounds volumes,

181
00:11:13.679 --> 00:11:17.679
I'm not getting more recast nav mesh data.

182
00:11:17.679 --> 00:11:24.129
That's because this is our global idea of our navigable space

183
00:11:24.129 --> 00:11:30.129
and the volumes represent each of the spaces that we want to demark as navigable space.

184
00:11:30.129 --> 00:11:32.129
This is basically our paintbrush.

185
00:11:32.129 --> 00:11:36.129
This is actually really the thing that represents our runtime data

186
00:11:36.129 --> 00:11:43.559
of the fully built-up picture of all our navigable space.

187
00:11:43.559 --> 00:11:51.559
Once we've got this nav mesh, there's a couple of different settings we can tweak on it.

188
00:11:51.559 --> 00:11:57.559
The volumes again are just there to define areas that should be considered for navigation.

189
00:11:57.559 --> 00:12:03.559
Then it's up to the settings on our recast nav mesh

190
00:12:03.559 --> 00:12:07.559
to define various things about how that nav mesh is being generated.

191
00:12:07.559 --> 00:12:12.559
There are settings in here such as being able to tweak our resolutions,

192
00:12:12.559 --> 00:12:14.559
being able to tweak our agent radius.

193
00:12:14.559 --> 00:12:17.559
We can tweak our cell sizes.

194
00:12:17.559 --> 00:12:28.399
If I were to put a block, if I were just to create a cone or something,

195
00:12:28.399 --> 00:12:36.500
no idea where that cone just went, just going to try making that again.

196
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:40.500
I don't know why it's suddenly having an issue creating.

197
00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:43.500
I'm going to try one more. I'm just going to try a cube.

198
00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.500
For some reason it likes cubes and doesn't like cylinders.

199
00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:55.500
If I were to go to this nav mesh and then I can go to my tile size.

200
00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.879
I always forget exactly.

201
00:12:58.879 --> 00:13:07.720
Tile size on real units, is that going to be right?

202
00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:21.360
If I change that to 50, it's not in our bounds volume.

203
00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.769
It is in the recast nav mesh.

204
00:13:24.769 --> 00:13:30.769
Oh, it's our cell sizes. There we go.

205
00:13:30.769 --> 00:13:39.759
We could change our cell size from 38 down to something like 1.

206
00:13:39.759 --> 00:13:44.759
If I change that on all our settings, you can see it regenerates the nav mesh

207
00:13:44.759 --> 00:13:55.179
and we end up with a nav mesh that more closely mimics or matches our geometry.

208
00:13:55.179 --> 00:14:01.179
The fact that there's a gap between our cube here is down to our agent radius.

209
00:14:01.179 --> 00:14:06.179
The whole point is this recast nav mesh is bound to a particular kind of agent

210
00:14:06.179 --> 00:14:11.179
and therefore it's generating the space that that kind of agent can navigate

211
00:14:11.179 --> 00:14:19.440
and therefore because our agent radius is whatever it is, 35 by default,

212
00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:25.440
this is going to be like a 35 centimeter gap between the, or 35 on real units,

213
00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.440
which is essentially centimeters gap between the geometry.

214
00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:34.399
So if I were to shrink that, you'd see kind of more closely hugs the geometry.

215
00:14:34.399 --> 00:14:39.399
And similarly, again, because this is all these nav mesh bounds volumes are,

216
00:14:39.399 --> 00:14:43.399
is to say look everywhere you could possibly here to generate nav.

217
00:14:43.399 --> 00:14:46.399
It's also generating it on top of spaces.

218
00:14:46.399 --> 00:14:48.399
It doesn't necessarily mean they'll be able to get here.

219
00:14:48.399 --> 00:14:51.399
There's no connection between these two islands,

220
00:14:51.399 --> 00:14:55.399
but these are both treated as independently possible navigable space.

221
00:14:55.399 --> 00:14:59.399
If I did want to connect this, we can talk about nav proxy links,

222
00:14:59.399 --> 00:15:00.399
which you can use in a little bit.

223
00:15:00.399 --> 00:15:07.559
Or the other thing we could do is introduce a slope to kind of be able to navigate up there

224
00:15:07.559 --> 00:15:14.559
because one of the things we can also define in this nav mesh is our agent max slope.

225
00:15:14.559 --> 00:15:21.559
And we can see if I were to, for example, duplicate that cube.

226
00:15:21.559 --> 00:15:27.559
And you can see it's actually going to try, and if I just scale this up a little bit,

227
00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:29.559
you can see it starts generating mesh.

228
00:15:29.559 --> 00:15:32.559
And now that I've kind of got an acceptable slope linking those two things,

229
00:15:32.559 --> 00:15:36.559
it's made one coherent navigable space.

230
00:15:36.559 --> 00:15:39.970
So that's how we create nav volumes.

231
00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:43.970
It's worth looking through everything available in your recast nav mesh

232
00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:45.970
to see the different settings available.

233
00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.970
If you're wondering why there are two recast nav meshes,

234
00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:51.970
that's because I have two profiles set up for different kind of agents.

235
00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:53.970
We'll get to those profiles in a little bit.

236
00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:56.970
But typically, if you just make a new project out of the box,

237
00:15:56.970 --> 00:16:00.970
you would only see one, and it would probably be called recast nav mesh default.

238
00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:02.970
I think that's the default agent profile name.

239
00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:07.970
But yeah, it's worth looking at those recast nav mesh details

240
00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:12.679
to kind of see what's adjustable there.

241
00:16:12.679 --> 00:16:14.679
Oh, actually, just whilst we're on that,

242
00:16:14.679 --> 00:16:16.679
one last thing that I should mention is,

243
00:16:16.679 --> 00:16:20.679
it's also in this nav mesh data that you would be choosing

244
00:16:20.679 --> 00:16:24.679
whether or not this data is static or dynamic.

245
00:16:24.679 --> 00:16:28.679
So I think it's one of the settings right at the bottom.

246
00:16:28.679 --> 00:16:32.679
You have a setting here, runtime generation.

247
00:16:32.679 --> 00:16:36.679
So by default, this nav mesh is going to be static,

248
00:16:36.679 --> 00:16:40.679
meaning it's not going to respond to runtime changes.

249
00:16:40.679 --> 00:16:44.679
Whereas if you were to say dynamic on dynamic modifiers only,

250
00:16:44.679 --> 00:16:48.679
we'll talk about nav modifiers in a little bit,

251
00:16:48.679 --> 00:16:52.679
but dynamic is going to be this can respond to runtime changes

252
00:16:52.679 --> 00:16:55.679
and dynamic modifiers only is going to be it's mostly static,

253
00:16:55.679 --> 00:17:00.679
but specifically nav modifiers can have a dynamic impact on this nav mesh.

254
00:17:00.679 --> 00:17:04.680
So that's a particularly important setting to be aware of

255
00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:08.680
if you need dynamic navigable spaces.

256
00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:10.680
You just know that that setting is there

257
00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:11.680
and that's how you would change that.

258
00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:19.109
And that is in the recast nav mesh data, not the nav volumes.

259
00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:21.109
Yep, so that's just a little preview

260
00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:22.109
of what a nav mesh might look like.

261
00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:24.109
And again, similarly, you can see here,

262
00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:26.109
because this has geometry

263
00:17:26.109 --> 00:17:30.109
and because this geometry is marked up to affect navigation,

264
00:17:30.109 --> 00:17:32.109
that's why it's carving out a volume.

265
00:17:32.109 --> 00:17:35.109
This cube is either too far above the nav mesh

266
00:17:35.109 --> 00:17:39.109
or it's not marked to affect navigation.

267
00:17:39.109 --> 00:17:41.109
We can actually see that effect very quickly here.

268
00:17:41.109 --> 00:17:44.109
If I were to do can affect,

269
00:17:44.109 --> 00:17:45.109
you can see there's this tick box here.

270
00:17:45.109 --> 00:17:49.109
This is going to be true of pretty much any primitive components.

271
00:17:49.109 --> 00:17:54.109
So that's going to be box volumes, static meshes, anything like that.

272
00:17:54.109 --> 00:17:55.109
It's going to have that setting

273
00:17:55.109 --> 00:17:57.109
and you can see as soon as I tick that off,

274
00:17:57.109 --> 00:17:59.109
it's no longer considering this mesh.

275
00:17:59.109 --> 00:18:01.109
It's going to generate navigable space underneath it.

276
00:18:01.109 --> 00:18:04.109
It doesn't mean the actors won't collide with this geometry,

277
00:18:04.109 --> 00:18:07.109
but certainly the navigation system isn't going to consider it

278
00:18:07.109 --> 00:18:10.109
for ignoring, take that into account

279
00:18:10.109 --> 00:18:12.109
when generating the navigable space.

280
00:18:12.109 --> 00:18:14.779
So agent profiles I kind of already mentioned,

281
00:18:14.779 --> 00:18:17.779
but I'll just quickly show those off.

282
00:18:17.779 --> 00:18:19.779
As we said, by default,

283
00:18:19.779 --> 00:18:21.779
there'll just be one profile set up for you.

284
00:18:21.779 --> 00:18:25.779
But I believe if you go into your edit project settings

285
00:18:25.779 --> 00:18:30.779
and into engine navigation system,

286
00:18:30.779 --> 00:18:32.779
we have supported agents.

287
00:18:32.779 --> 00:18:35.779
So again, if you have a game

288
00:18:35.779 --> 00:18:38.779
where you need different kinds of entities

289
00:18:38.779 --> 00:18:41.779
that can navigate the space in different ways,

290
00:18:41.779 --> 00:18:46.779
that's a really good candidate for using multiple kinds of agents.

291
00:18:46.779 --> 00:18:49.779
And you essentially would just create a new agent here,

292
00:18:49.779 --> 00:18:51.779
give the agent a name,

293
00:18:51.779 --> 00:18:54.779
and then you can just define settings about its size.

294
00:18:54.779 --> 00:18:58.779
So how, what would the default radius for this agent be?

295
00:18:58.779 --> 00:19:01.779
Height for this agent be?

296
00:19:01.779 --> 00:19:05.779
Step height, so if we're considering a child versus an adult,

297
00:19:05.779 --> 00:19:09.779
they might be able to treat different vertical differences in geometry

298
00:19:09.779 --> 00:19:12.779
as kind of acceptable to navigate over.

299
00:19:12.779 --> 00:19:16.779
And so you can define a height radius.

300
00:19:16.779 --> 00:19:20.779
You can even define whether or not this particular agent can jump at all,

301
00:19:20.779 --> 00:19:22.779
whether they can crouch.

302
00:19:22.779 --> 00:19:24.779
That can affect the nav generation.

303
00:19:24.779 --> 00:19:27.779
And you can also create your own nav mesh classes.

304
00:19:27.779 --> 00:19:33.779
So a particular agent can basically define a particular nav mesh data class

305
00:19:33.779 --> 00:19:37.779
is used when creating the nav mesh for that agent.

306
00:19:37.779 --> 00:19:40.779
So I've actually, just in the C++ for this project,

307
00:19:40.779 --> 00:19:43.779
I've just done a very simple example.

308
00:19:43.779 --> 00:19:45.779
I've not actually done anything with it,

309
00:19:45.779 --> 00:19:50.779
but I've just created a new class that inherits from recast nav mesh

310
00:19:50.779 --> 00:19:53.779
just to kind of show that you can do this.

311
00:19:53.779 --> 00:19:55.779
That does require, if you were to do this,

312
00:19:55.779 --> 00:19:59.779
you just need to bear in mind that in your build CS,

313
00:19:59.779 --> 00:20:02.779
you do need to include the navigation system

314
00:20:02.779 --> 00:20:07.779
because that's what will contain the nav mesh actor.

315
00:20:07.779 --> 00:20:09.779
But if you create a separate class,

316
00:20:09.779 --> 00:20:15.779
you can now choose a preferred nav mesh data that actor will use.

317
00:20:15.779 --> 00:20:17.779
And again, I think as this says,

318
00:20:17.779 --> 00:20:21.779
you can also specify none and then it will just choose any nav data it doesn't mind.

319
00:20:21.779 --> 00:20:23.779
And you can also say for this particular agent,

320
00:20:23.779 --> 00:20:27.779
specifically generate a nav mesh using this class.

321
00:20:27.779 --> 00:20:31.809
So that way you can have agents that require a particular class type

322
00:20:31.809 --> 00:20:35.809
and other agents that can use nav mesh data of that type,

323
00:20:35.809 --> 00:20:41.809
but also can tolerate multiple types.

324
00:20:41.809 --> 00:20:43.809
And so when you create those different agents,

325
00:20:43.809 --> 00:20:47.809
one thing to bear in mind is that if you don't have different settings,

326
00:20:47.809 --> 00:20:51.809
if you don't either have like different agent heights or different agent radius,

327
00:20:51.809 --> 00:20:53.809
when you go to drag those volumes in,

328
00:20:53.809 --> 00:20:57.809
you may not end up with multiple recast nav meshes.

329
00:20:57.809 --> 00:20:59.809
So if you create different agents

330
00:20:59.809 --> 00:21:02.809
and it seems like you're only ever getting one recast nav mesh data,

331
00:21:02.809 --> 00:21:04.809
you actually need to make sure that there are meaningful differences

332
00:21:04.809 --> 00:21:07.809
between your agents, otherwise Unreal will,

333
00:21:07.809 --> 00:21:10.809
I guess, just infer that they're so similar

334
00:21:10.809 --> 00:21:14.809
that they can share a recast nav mesh data.

335
00:21:14.809 --> 00:21:16.809
So just make sure that there are actually meaningful differences

336
00:21:16.809 --> 00:21:18.809
between your agents such as different radius

337
00:21:18.809 --> 00:21:20.809
or different heights or something like that.

338
00:21:20.809 --> 00:21:23.809
Otherwise you won't get multiple recast nav meshes.

339
00:21:23.809 --> 00:21:26.809
And then for each of your bounds volumes,

340
00:21:26.809 --> 00:21:34.039
you can then say, is this bounds volume only for agents of a particular type?

341
00:21:34.039 --> 00:21:39.039
So there will be a tick box for each of the agent profiles that you've defined.

342
00:21:39.039 --> 00:21:41.039
The default is that it supports all,

343
00:21:41.039 --> 00:21:45.039
but if this nav mesh should only be generated for the common one

344
00:21:45.039 --> 00:21:47.039
rather than the big one, I can tick that off

345
00:21:47.039 --> 00:21:50.039
and you can see that gets regenerated.

346
00:21:50.039 --> 00:21:55.039
And then that nav mesh data, this whole area will only be navigable

347
00:21:55.039 --> 00:22:01.039
by agents using the common agent type rather than the big agent type.

348
00:22:01.039 --> 00:22:03.039
So that's kind of just another thing to be aware of

349
00:22:03.039 --> 00:22:06.220
when we're dealing with agent profiles.

350
00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:08.220
So nav link proxies, as I've mentioned,

351
00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:14.220
give us a way to connect independent areas.

352
00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:18.220
The first example would be something like jumps.

353
00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:23.220
It gives us a way to jump from a ledge back down to the rest of the graph,

354
00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:28.220
but it also gives us a way to connect up isolated islands of space.

355
00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:33.220
So if I were to have two spaces like this,

356
00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:37.220
the way you would do this is there is an actor called a nav link proxy.

357
00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:41.220
I can never remember if you can find it through the all tab.

358
00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:46.539
If I go nav link proxy, that's finding a blueprint that I've made.

359
00:22:46.539 --> 00:22:50.539
Yeah, it doesn't for some reason, but there is probably another way to do this.

360
00:22:50.539 --> 00:22:54.539
But you can also just go to your all.

361
00:22:54.539 --> 00:22:58.799
And if I just search nav link proxy, you can find that here.

362
00:22:58.799 --> 00:22:59.799
And then this is an actor.

363
00:22:59.799 --> 00:23:03.799
This is essentially what a nav link is, is this actor.

364
00:23:03.799 --> 00:23:06.799
So you would drag this round into world.

365
00:23:06.799 --> 00:23:10.799
And essentially you just need to arrange this appropriately

366
00:23:10.799 --> 00:23:15.799
such that it actually links these two bits of geometry.

367
00:23:15.799 --> 00:23:19.799
So if I were to quickly demonstrate this,

368
00:23:19.799 --> 00:23:23.799
in fact, what I'll do is I've got an example map.

369
00:23:23.799 --> 00:23:28.799
We'll revisit this example map because it's demonstrating a couple of different things at once.

370
00:23:28.799 --> 00:23:32.799
But if I just go to our example map,

371
00:23:32.799 --> 00:23:36.799
we've got kind of two islands of space here, right?

372
00:23:36.799 --> 00:23:40.049
We have these two navigable spaces.

373
00:23:40.049 --> 00:23:45.049
And if I've kind of set up some stuff in the level blueprint

374
00:23:45.049 --> 00:23:51.049
that essentially just says when I press the three button order,

375
00:23:51.049 --> 00:23:57.049
this AI to try and move to where this point is.

376
00:23:57.049 --> 00:24:01.049
So if I just play and hit three, you can see it tries to get there,

377
00:24:01.049 --> 00:24:02.049
but it doesn't get very far.

378
00:24:02.049 --> 00:24:03.049
It gets to about here.

379
00:24:03.049 --> 00:24:08.049
It gets to the edge of its navigable space before it's like,

380
00:24:08.049 --> 00:24:09.049
well, I just can't reach that point anymore.

381
00:24:09.049 --> 00:24:12.049
I'm as close as I possibly can be, but I can't reach there.

382
00:24:12.049 --> 00:24:16.470
So if we put in, I've got a pre-made nav proxy here.

383
00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:19.470
If I just kind of snap this down to the floor,

384
00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:20.470
you can see the nav mesh regenerates.

385
00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:25.470
And that's because I've made a kind of like a link between these two islands.

386
00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:29.470
This is a specific actor that I've made,

387
00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:31.470
but really this just inherits from nav link proxy.

388
00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:34.470
I'll explain why I've made that nav link in a minute,

389
00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:37.470
why I've made that actor in a second.

390
00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:42.470
But just for now, you can see now that I've connected,

391
00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:46.470
now that I've brought that nav link down and kind of connected it up with the mesh,

392
00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:50.470
it's now like treated as a kind of valid connection.

393
00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:54.559
So if I were to kind of lift that up and away,

394
00:24:54.559 --> 00:24:56.559
I don't know how close it has to be exactly,

395
00:24:56.559 --> 00:25:01.559
but you can kind of see the arrow gets generated right when it's close enough,

396
00:25:01.559 --> 00:25:07.250
when it's no longer close enough.

397
00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.250
That's just hanging around now.

398
00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:15.599
If I just want to play, sorry, just clicking the wrong things.

399
00:25:15.599 --> 00:25:18.599
Yeah, you can see that that's, for some reason,

400
00:25:18.599 --> 00:25:21.599
the debug arrow is kind of hanging around there,

401
00:25:21.599 --> 00:25:23.599
but that's no longer navigable space.

402
00:25:23.599 --> 00:25:28.599
And there's a certain point at which this is now kind of like,

403
00:25:28.599 --> 00:25:30.599
and you can see the nav mesh reacting to it, right?

404
00:25:30.599 --> 00:25:32.599
So it's too far away,

405
00:25:32.599 --> 00:25:37.599
won't be treated as kind of an acceptable link

406
00:25:37.599 --> 00:25:41.170
and once we're close enough, now it is.

407
00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:47.170
So the way you work with a nav link proxy is you,

408
00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:49.170
again, this is just an actor you can drag into the level.

409
00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:51.170
You can make blueprints that inherit from it,

410
00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:53.170
as you can see that I've done.

411
00:25:53.170 --> 00:25:56.400
And then essentially it's just these two point links.

412
00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:00.400
A nav link can also represent multiple links at one time.

413
00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:04.400
So it's not just a one to one.

414
00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:07.400
You can have a nav link that kind of represents a web of connections.

415
00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:10.400
But by default, it's going to be just two points.

416
00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.400
And it's got like a kind of left point and a right point.

417
00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.400
And you essentially just manually author these points.

418
00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:18.400
So you'll see, as I change these values,

419
00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:21.400
I've got this, you can literally see the connection changing.

420
00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:26.400
So you would just, if this is meant to represent a jump,

421
00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:29.400
obviously you could just manually author this to be like,

422
00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:31.400
have a certain amount of vertical transition between them.

423
00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:34.400
And that's kind of all there is to it.

424
00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:38.400
Then you can similarly mark up here, which agents is it suitable for?

425
00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:41.400
How far should it project up and down?

426
00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:43.400
So that's essentially going to be like,

427
00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:46.400
you know, if it's a little bit above the nav mesh,

428
00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:49.400
is it still going to try and find a nav mesh within this much vertical distance

429
00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:51.400
that it can be a suitable link for?

430
00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:56.400
And then also this idea that the, you know, is it a nav link?

431
00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:58.400
Is it one directional?

432
00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:02.400
So something like a jump, obviously you shouldn't be able to navigate back upwards up that cliff.

433
00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:03.400
It's kind of one directional.

434
00:27:03.400 --> 00:27:07.400
So you can choose, is this a nav link that is suitable for both directions,

435
00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:09.400
or is it left to right or right to left?

436
00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:13.400
And I suspect we'll get an update in the arrow that kind of demonstrates that,

437
00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:16.400
that it will kind of only work that one way.

438
00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.400
Now, the other thing to mention about this,

439
00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:21.400
and this is the kind of like reason I've made a blueprint here,

440
00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:24.400
is we've got this kind of more complicated form of nav links,

441
00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:27.400
of nav link proxies, which is called smart links.

442
00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:32.400
So with simple nav links, with the kind of standard nav link,

443
00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:36.400
that's kind of static data that is built in at edit time.

444
00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:40.400
If we need something that is a little bit more nuanced, a little bit more dynamic,

445
00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:42.400
that's where smart links come in.

446
00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:45.400
Smart links basically give us the same capabilities of nav link.

447
00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:47.400
They are part of the nav link proxy,

448
00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:56.230
but they give us the ability to essentially turn this functionality on and off.

449
00:27:56.230 --> 00:28:00.230
If a nav link proxy is utilizing,

450
00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:05.230
because again, any nav link proxy actor you make is kind of both at the same time.

451
00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:08.230
They've got the simple link and the smart link category.

452
00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:13.230
If it is a smart link as well as a normal link,

453
00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:18.230
then it will prefer to use the smart link if they seem to have the same connections.

454
00:28:18.230 --> 00:28:24.230
You can copy the kind of functionality of the basic points over to the smart link.

455
00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:28.230
When you look in the smart link section,

456
00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:31.230
we've got a link relative start and a link relative end.

457
00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:34.230
This works in exactly the same way that our left and right do here.

458
00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:37.230
But if you've authored a simple link,

459
00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:39.230
then you need to convert it to a smart link.

460
00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:43.230
If I change this connection a little bit here,

461
00:28:43.230 --> 00:28:45.230
you'll see there's just this handy editor button

462
00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:49.230
that essentially just copies that left and right data

463
00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:52.230
into our link relative start and link relative end.

464
00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:54.230
Otherwise, these do exactly the same job.

465
00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:56.230
You can see as I tweak this,

466
00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:59.230
you can see it's changing this kind of second set of data,

467
00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:03.230
and this represents the actual smart link, not the simple link.

468
00:29:03.230 --> 00:29:06.769
Smart links, the same functionality here.

469
00:29:06.769 --> 00:29:09.769
You can affect which directions it's for, whether it's both,

470
00:29:09.769 --> 00:29:13.769
whether it's one way or the other.

471
00:29:13.769 --> 00:29:17.769
You can check whether or not it's for a particular area, classes,

472
00:29:17.769 --> 00:29:19.769
and then you can turn this link on and off.

473
00:29:19.769 --> 00:29:21.769
You can see as I turn that off,

474
00:29:21.769 --> 00:29:25.769
we get this different arrow color, so this is no longer a usable link.

475
00:29:25.769 --> 00:29:27.769
Obviously, in this situation,

476
00:29:27.769 --> 00:29:31.769
because we've also got a setup here for our default point links,

477
00:29:31.769 --> 00:29:32.769
then it's kind of both.

478
00:29:32.769 --> 00:29:34.769
But if I were just to delete that,

479
00:29:34.769 --> 00:29:36.769
this is only a smart link.

480
00:29:36.769 --> 00:29:38.769
This is not usable,

481
00:29:38.769 --> 00:29:41.769
so our actor would simply not be able to use it.

482
00:29:41.769 --> 00:29:47.250
And if I were to set that as enabled, now it can.

